  "result": "The O3 lesson is written, with a quiz and a cheat sheet. The two reviews both ended on \"revise\", so some reviewer findings are still open.\n\nThe lesson is `lessons/0001-context-and-memory.html`, a 30-minute lesson. It covers:\n- what the context window holds on one call;\n- how the program re-sends the history between steps, with a worked token trace;\n- clearing and compaction, so the window stays small;\n- notes as memory between sessions, using a student's training status;\n- retrieval for finding the right page in a long manual;\n- a closing exercise that sorts items into window, notes or retrieval.\n\nThe token counts in the traces are invented round numbers, and the lesson says so. The sources are four Anthropic pages, now listed in `RESOURCES.md`. I opened them and they support the claims they're cited for.\n\nI changed the example student's name to \"Sam\", since \"Riley\" is your name.\n\n**Still open from the reviews:**\n- In Parts 1 to 4 the definition comes before the example, not after it as `PREFERENCES.md` asks.\n- Part 5's worked example packs in several search ideas at once.\n- The lesson runs about 4,900 words, which is inside the 30-minute range but dense.\n- No community link is included, because I didn't find one I could verify.\n\nThe review record is `review-records/0001-lesson.md`, and `NOTES.md` has the same open points.\n\nQuiz 0002 (five items) is ready in `quizzes/0002.json` whenever you want to take it. The cheat sheet is `cheatsheets/context-and-memory.html`. Ask me about anything in the lesson that's unclear, and I can redo any part shorter and simpler.",
